.Dd August 27, 2026
.Dt C4-USE 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm c4-use
.Nd using external code in C4
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Ic use
.Op Cm - | Cm ~ | Cm +
.Pf < Pa library.c4a Ns >

.Ic use
.Op Cm - | Cm ~
.Qq Pa source.c4
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The C4 language allows both the use of external libraries and referencing files
within the same
.Dq project
or
.Dq target .
Both cases of using happen with the adequately named
.Ic use
keyword which is then followed by an optional visibility marker
.Pq see Sx Visibility
and either a
.Qq quoted
or <bracketed> filename. The former is for
.Sx Source inclusion
whereas the latter is used for
.Sx Library inclusion .
Effectively they both perform the same action: allow code to be reused from
another source, be it a local file or a library someone already packaged.

Conventionally, one would put
.Ic use
keywords at the top of the file, but it is not required. The contents therein
will, however, only be visible in following expressions. They can be nested on
the same line as well if separated with some whitespace. In other words, they
act as arity-1 functions, but are specially handled.

When using external files, the functions referenced are made available only
within the scope wherein the
.Ic use
keyword was used. This makes it possible to only
.Dq locally
\(en within a function body \(en pull in some dependency. See
.Xr BUGS
however.
.\"
.Ss Source inclusion
When using local files with the
.Ic use
.Qq quoted
syntax, the referenced file is expected to be a fully valid C4 source file that
can be read and understood by the compiler. Doing this makes all visible
functions declared within the used file callable from the user file.

The whole file is not parsed, however, only top-level expressions. Most
importantly, all blocks are thrown away as-is which makes the use-parser faster
than the actual parser at the cost of it allowing code that is invalid. But
those have to be compiled by themselves anyhow, which makes this not an issue in
general.

Source files are not really looked up, more so the used file's name is simply
interpreted as a relative path from the directory of the user source itself.
.\"
.Ss Library inclusion
When using libraries with the
.Ic use
<bracketed>
syntax, the referenced file must be a
.Bx
or SysV/GNU
.Xr ar 1
archive that contains at least one object file
.Pq real or synthetic
that contains a section named
.Sy .c4xport
with a serialized listing
.Pq for more information on the format see Xr c4-archive 7
of all exported symbols. Conveniently, the
.Xr c4c 1
compiler generates such sections for functions with +public visibility, and the
.Xr c4ar 1
archive utility concatenates all of them into one synthetic object. Other,
non-C4 generated object files (eg. from C) could be part of this archive as well
as all other contents are ignored.

When at least one library is referenced in this manner by a file, a section in
the object file named
.Sy .c4mport
is generated with the name of the used library file
.Pq see Xr c4-archive 7 for format information .
This is later used by the linker to trace transitive library dependencies
automatically.

Library lookup is performed by using a
.Dq search path
within each tool that is consulted for looking up libraries. This is uniformly
handled across all of these tools. The argument path is then searched in order
as a relative path to any of these entries, stopping at the first. By default,
the C4 tools use the following paths on
.Ux Ns -ish
operating systems, relative to the installation prefix:
.Pp
.Bl -inset -compact
.It Pf < Pa prefix Ns > Ns / Ns Pa lib
.It Pf < Pa prefix Ns > Ns / Ns Pa lib64
.El
.Pp
Using the
.Fl I
flag on any of these tools, the user can modify this list. Passing it the
.Cm -
value completely resets it to an empty list. Giving it a path will append it to
the current list. This allows using custom non system-wide installation
locations to be found by
.Ic use
expressions.
.\"
.Ss Visibility
The
.Ic use
keyword accepts an optional visibility modifier to how control external code can
see the file the current file uses. This is syntactically the same
.Po
.Cm + ,
.Cm - ,
or
.Cm ~
usually referred to as +public, -private, or ~internal for clarity while keeping
the read flow of text.
.Pc
as used for declaring function visibility within a file, but with a different
default value
.Pq -private .

By default, all
.Ic use
actions are of
.Em -private
visibility. This means that external code that uses this source file will
.Em NOT
see any functions that would be transitively used from the files this source
file uses. This is defensive as to not inadvertently introduce transitive
dependencies as in with C source file
.Ic #include
preprocessor directives.

The more liberal
.Em ~internal
visibility allows propagating uses to the file's users. For example, assume that
file
.Pa A.c4
uses \(en with an ~internal modifier \(en file
.Pa B.c4 .
Then the main entry point named
.Pa main.c4
uses only
.Pa A.c4 .
Because, however,
.Pa A.c4
used its own dependency with an ~internal modifier, the final user gets the
functions declared in
.Em both
files, whereas if it only included it with a -private modifier, then
.Pa main.c4
could only see the functions declared in
.Pa A.c4 .
This can be used to create facade use files, that allow subsystems to be easily
used in other parts of a project, without factoring it out into a custom,
self managed library.

For library uses only the
.Em +public
visibility can be specified
.Po
.Ic use
+<bracketed>
.Pc
as well. This propagates similarly to the ~internal
discussed above, but it also escapes to the library interface. That is, this
object will then export all symbols that the library it used exported. This can
be used when building extra tools around libraries so that users don't have to
explicitly use the wrapped library.
.\"
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr c4ar 1 ,
.Xr c4-archive 5
.Sh AUTHORS
.An András Bodor Aq Mt bodand@proton.me
.Sh BUGS
Scoped usage can only happen once per file, because the compiler does not
properly deduplicate those functions, thus it will try to link with functions
that were deduplicated by LLVM (ie. appended with a unique suffix), which then,
of course, do not exist. As a consequence, if a source has two or more functions
that would use the same dependency, it needs to be hoisted to a file-level use
expression.

While the
.Ic use
keyword itself introduces an expression as-is, that would have a normal value,
currently it is treated as the empty expression
.Pq Ic ";"
which has weird properties especially in function calls. Error messages are
similarly bogus referencing things like argument cutting when using
.Ic use
in a function parameter
.Pq for whatever reason .

The search path is not properly defined for
.Pf non- Ux Ns -ish
operating systems. Particularly Windows, which has a completely different
directory structure.
